#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111824 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might need to use more than one rule of exponents to simplify an expression. First, let's remind ourselves of all of the rules of exponents.\n\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nAs an example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(u^2 v\\right)^3\n$$\n\nWe start by using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n(u^{2}v)^{3} = (u^{2})^{3} \\cdot v^{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u^{2})^{3} \\cdot v^{3} &=  \\\\\nu^{(2 \\cdot 3)} \\cdot v^{3} &=  \\\\\nu^{6} \\cdot v^{3} &=  \\\\\n&= u^{6}v^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "13b1c64a-c868-5824-919e-c3a665bb4a96" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1485 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111904 :tutorial/content "To simplify the expression $x\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{x^2}$, we first rewrite the radical as a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\nx \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{x^2} = x \\cdot \\left(x^2 \\right)^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\nx \\cdot \\left(x^2 \\right)^{1/3} = x \\cdot x^{2(1/3)} = x \\cdot x^{2/3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx \\cdot x^{2/3} &=  \\\\\nx^{1 + 2/3} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{5/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\nx^{5/3} = \\sqrt[3]{x^5}\n$$\n\nNotice that, by combining the radicals as fractional exponents and power rules, we get the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "91debf57-af23-518b-b432-ea04beccc17a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6221 :tutorial/title "Rules of Exponents and Radicals"}]}
